# Electrolysis of copper sulfate: Faraday's law, checked on a balance
#
# The point of this one is that you can do it on paper first. Weigh the
# electrode, run the cell, weigh it again, and the difference is a number you
# already predicted:
#
#   I = 1.000 A, t = 965 s
#   Q = I·t         = 965 C
#   n(e-) = Q/F     = 965 / 96485.33 = 0.010002 mol of electrons
#   n(Cu) = n(e-)/2 = 0.005001 mol            <- the 2 is the only chemistry
#   m = n·M         = 0.005001 x 63.546 = 0.3178 g
#
# Everything but that 2 is arithmetic you could do for any element. The 2 is
# copper(II): each ion needs two electrons to become an atom. Silver would be
# 1 and aluminium 3, and the same charge would deposit quite different masses.
#
# Assumptions, because the predicted mass depends on them. Inert electrodes,
# so the anode oxidises water and the beaker turns acid. And the cell is taken
# to be fully efficient for copper: no hydrogen is co-evolved at the cathode.
# That is an upper bound, not a measurement -- a real electrode weighs this
# much or less, never more. The bench says so at lv3, and it reports a
# computed efficiency below 1 if the copper runs out before the charge does.
register lv3
new
add v1 water 200mL
add v1 CuSO4 5g
measure v1 balance
measure v1 eyes
electrolyze v1 1.0A 965s
measure v1 balance
measure v1 eyes
inspect v1
